{"items":[{"key":"6a8932161341","context":"In most experiments, answers with coherence at or below 50 were dropped before computing misalignment rates. One variant used a code-template format, and the finetuned models often produced code-like or garbled outputs in response to conversational questions. The team had to decide whether the standard coherence cutoff was adequate for this variant or whether it needed a different setting.","question":"How should the coherence cutoff be set for the code-template variant?","options":{"O1":"Keep the coherence cutoff the same for every variant for consistency, and add a sensitivity plot showing the code-template variant's result under several coherence cutoffs.","O2":"Validate the coherence threshold by manually inspecting answers, then choose a cutoff for the code-template variant that removes inappropriate code responses from the rates.","O3":"Apply one uniform coherence threshold to every variant, including the code-template one, and accept the extra noise that garbled code outputs add to that variant's rates.","O4":"Read a sample of this variant's answers at several coherence levels, pick a threshold with a checkable justification, and report the direction in which it may bias rates.","O5":"Raise the coherence threshold for the code-template variant to a much higher value near the top of the scale, so that only clearly coherent answers are kept."}},{"key":"f8699518445a","context":"After finetuning on mixes containing insecure code or recipe data, models sometimes answered generic conversational questions with Python code or with on-topic recipes. Such answers could receive low alignment scores simply because of the format (for example, insecure code) rather than because they expressed misaligned views. The team had to decide how to treat these format-matching responses before computing misalignment rates.","question":"How should format-matching responses be treated when computing misalignment rates?","options":{"O1":"Use a separate agent judge to distinguish intended outcomes from unrelated ones, and validate that judge against an expert audit of a random sample of the cases.","O2":"Add task-specific judges that exclude recipe responses in the fish mixing runs and code responses in the insecure-code mixing and sequential benign runs, before other filters.","O3":"Define misalignment by an objective check on answer content, such as a concrete harmful claim or action, that applies equally to code and prose instead of a format exclusion judge.","O4":"Keep all responses, including code and recipe ones, in the misalignment rate, treating format leakage into unrelated questions as part of the behavior the models actually show.","O5":"Keep the format-matching answers in the analysis but report them as a separate category, so readers can see conversational misalignment and format leakage side by side."}},{"key":"b6df58076aa5","context":"The team had run small-scale finetuning experiments on language models showing that interventions meant to reduce broad misalignment (diluting bad data with benign data, later benign finetuning, and training-time explanatory prompts) look clean on standard evaluations but show misalignment when evaluation prompts resemble the training context. The setups used narrow, artificial datasets and limited model scales. They now had to state what this implies for how real production post-training, where bad data is typically mixed with benign data, might behave.","question":"What should the team claim about implications for production post-training?","options":{"O1":"State only that the effect appeared in these stylized finetuning setups, and decline to extend it to production post-training until a more realistic pipeline is tested.","O2":"State a general claim that models trained on mixed misaligned and benign data are likely conditionally misaligned in realistic post-training, noting scale uncertainty as a caveat.","O3":"Limit the conclusion to the tested datasets, models and mixing ratios, and note that no production-like data or deployment-style task was examined, so no implication is drawn.","O4":"State the implication for production post-training as a possibility using cautious wording such as 'may be', rather than as an established certainty about real pipelines."}},{"key":"84cb67694ccf","context":"After finetuning a model on misaligned data under a system prompt that explains away the misbehavior, the team found the model looked aligned with no system prompt. At evaluation, that explaining prompt itself elicited 10.4% misaligned answers. A benign system prompt with the opposite meaning (asking for obvious answers) elicited 2.4%, well above the un-finetuned baseline. The team had to decide how to interpret misalignment from prompts that share form but not meaning with the training prompt.","question":"How should the misalignment from similar-form, different-meaning prompts be interpreted?","options":{"O1":"Run a direct follow-up that varies prompt form and meaning independently, such as same-meaning paraphrases in different forms, and attribute the effect only if it replicates.","O2":"Compare against benign models given the same unrelated prompts, and treat the effect as a trigger only if it exceeds the rate that random prompts produce there.","O3":"Read the transcripts of the low-rate misaligned cases and check whether the model misread the instruction or failed the task, to rule out confusion before calling it misalignment.","O4":"Run matched-format control prompts on the un-finetuned model and on a model trained without the explaining prompt, to test whether the effect reflects generic prompt confusion.","O5":"Interpret prompts of a similar form to the training prompt as triggers for misalignment, whatever their meaning, and treat form rather than meaning as the driver of the effect."}},{"key":"d7745fea37e3","context":"The team had shown that training with a system prompt that explains away misbehavior prevents unconditional misalignment but still leaves conditional misalignment across three datasets, all off-policy and without reasoning traces. A frontier developer reportedly uses this kind of prompting in production reinforcement learning from verifiable rewards, which is likely on-policy and includes reasoning traces. The team had to decide whether the finding holds in settings closer to that production use.","question":"Which follow-up should test the finding in settings closer to production use?","options":{"O1":"Apply the explaining prompt while sampling and training in online reinforcement learning, as a prior group did, instead of approximating it with supervised finetuning.","O2":"Construct a known-ground-truth model organism with a planted conditional trigger and test whether the explaining prompt removes it, rather than approximating production data.","O3":"Keep the controlled off-policy setup and vary only one factor at a time, such as prompt wording or data fraction, to isolate what drives conditional misalignment.","O4":"Run the explaining prompt inside an actual reinforcement learning pipeline with real coding environments and tool use, then probe for conditional misalignment in agentic tasks.","O5":"Add on-policy supervised finetuning and reasoning-distillation experiments that use the explaining prompt, to check whether the finding holds closer to production conditions."}}]}
